HAIL



Hail = balls of ICE that grow inside a thunderstorm.



 Normal rain:     drop forms ──► falls ──► rain on the ground
 Hail:            drop forms ──► carried UP ──► freezes ──► falls ──► carried UP again
                                 (many times, it grows like an onion)



Hail can crack the windshield, dent the radome (nose), and hurt the engines.
It can fall OUT OF THE SIDE or the TOP (anvil) of a storm, into clear air,
a few NM away from the red area.




HOW HAIL IS MADE

  Step 1: STRONG UPDRAFT          Step 2: GOES ABOVE FREEZING      Step 3: TOO HEAVY, FALLS

      ▲ ▲ ▲  air goes up               ❄ ice ❄   ← cold (below 0 °C)       ●  ●  hail
      ▲ ▲ ▲  VERY fast                 ▲ ▲ ▲                                ↓  ↓
      ▲ ▲ ▲  (30+ m/s)          ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ ─  freezing level            ↓  ↓
      ▲ ▲ ▲  carries drops             ▲ ▲ ▲                                ↓  ↓
   ☁☁☁☁☁☁☁                          drops                             ══════════  ground


  The STRONGER the updraft  →  the HIGHER the water goes above the freezing level
                            →  the BIGGER the hail.

  So: strong echo FAR ABOVE the freezing level = strong updraft = HAIL.




HOW A REAL RADAR DETECTS IT

  A real aircraft radar CANNOT SEE HAIL. It only sees how strong the echo is.

        Wet hail (low, warm, water skin)        Dry hail (high, cold)
        ────────────────────────────────        ──────────────────────────
        VERY strong echo: red / magenta         WEAK echo: green or nothing
        → looks dangerous ✅                     → looks SAFE but is NOT ❗


                         Hail detection
                               │
             ┌─────────────────┴──────────────────┐
        PILOT (older radars)                 HAIL ICON (newer radars)
        looks for clues:                     Honeywell IntuVue, Collins MultiScan
             │                                     │
   - hook, finger, U-shape, scalloped     radar scans the storm at many heights
     edges (Airbus briefing note)         → strong echo high above the
   - red/magenta HIGH above freezing        freezing level?
     (tilt up, echo still there)          → draws a hail icon on the storm
   - storm grows fast                        │
                                          exact rule = company SECRET
                                          (not published)


  Ground radars (NEXRAD, USA) use a PUBLISHED rule (Waldvogel 1979):

     "45 dBZ echo reaching 4,600 ft (1.4 km) or MORE above the
      freezing level → good chance of hail"

        ─────────── 45 dBZ top ───────────┐
                                          │ 4,600 ft or more?  → HAIL
        ─ ─ ─ ─ ─ freezing level ─ ─ ─ ─ ─┘




WHAT MY APP DOES

 FlightGear (has NO hail at all)
        │  gives: rain volumes (strength, base, top) + clouds + temperature
        ▼
 YOUR APP (radar.py hail_cells)
        │
        ├─ Freezing level known?                         ── no ──► no hail icon
        │        yes
        ├─ Is it a RAIN volume?                          ── no ──► no hail icon
        │        yes
        ├─ With cloud shapes: is it a REAL STORM?        ── no ──► no hail icon
        │  (has a Cumulonimbus cloud)
        │        yes → storm top = highest cloud, icon goes on the storm CORE
        │
        ├─ Freezing level of THIS storm's area (weather stations) ✅
        │
        ├─ Go UP through the storm, 100 ft at a time:
        │     strength = rain  (rain 0.8 = 49 dBZ, rain 1.0 = 57 dBZ)
        │     above freezing level: ice → 7 dB weaker ✅
        │     Realistic mode: top of the storm fades ✅
        │
        ├─ Find the HIGHEST point that is still 45 dBZ or more
        │
        ├─ That point 4,600 ft or more above freezing?   ── no ──► no hail icon
        │        yes
        ▼
   HAIL ICON (white upside-down triangle with dots) on the storm
   shown in WX and WX+T, when the radar is sending, inside the screen




EXAMPLES (freezing level 12,000 ft, Realistic mode)


  Storm A: rain 0.8 (49 dBZ), top 35,000 ft

     35,000 ┐
            │   above freezing: 49 - 7 = 42 dBZ   (below 45)
     12,000 ┼ ─ ─ freezing ─ ─      highest 45 dBZ point = 12,000 ft
            │   49 dBZ              12,000 - 12,000 = 0 ft above freezing
          0 ┘                       → NO HAIL ❌


  Storm B: rain 0.9 (53 dBZ), top 35,000 ft

     35,000 ┐
     ~17,500│ ← 45 dBZ reaches up to here (53 - 7 = 46, fading with height)
     12,000 ┼ ─ ─ freezing ─ ─     about 5,500 ft above freezing  ≥ 4,600
          0 ┘                       → HAIL ✅


  Storm C: SAME rain 0.9, but top only 20,000 ft

     20,000 ┐
     ~13,900│ ← 45 dBZ only up to here (fades faster in a short storm)
     12,000 ┼ ─ ─ freezing ─ ─     about 1,900 ft above freezing  < 4,600
          0 ┘                       → NO HAIL ❌

  Same rain, different height → different answer. TALL strong storms = hail.


  Storm D: rain 1.0 (57 dBZ), top 35,000 ft

     above freezing: 57 - 7 = 50 dBZ → 45 dBZ reaches about 24,800 ft
     about 12,800 ft above freezing → HAIL ✅




IS IT CORRECT?

  Hail guessed from height of strong echo       ✅ (same idea as real hail icons,
                                                    published NEXRAD rule)
  Dry hail high up looks weak                   ✅ (ice -7 dB)
  Only real storms (Cumulonimbus)               ✅
  Freezing level per area                       ✅
  Wet hail extra strong (low down)              ❌ hail is treated like rain
  Hail icon on Simulated storms                 ❌ FlightGear volumes only
  METAR "GR" (hail reported)                    ⚠️ counted as rain
  Hail falling out of the anvil (clear air)     ❌ real radars don't show it either




